  <h2>The root contract</h2>
  <pre><code>.life                  # the root manifest — YAML head for machines, Markdown body for agents
.life.lock             # resolved imports — generated by the engine; commit it
.life.engine           # the engine binary — commit it; self-updates via evolve
.genome/               # vendored genes — gitignored, rematerialized each session
.claude/settings.json  # compiled harness settings — the one tracked harness file</code></pre>
  <p><code>.genome/</code> is the repo's <strong>genome</strong>: the genes it has inherited, vendored locally. It is build output — gitignored, and rematerialized from <code>.life.lock</code> every session by <code>life evolve</code>. The engine bundles no adapters; harness and infra adapters (<code>claude-code</code>, <code>cloudflare</code>, <code>github</code>, …) are genes like any other, loaded from the genome. Each adapter documents its own config — <code>life ask cloudflare</code>, <code>life ask claude-code</code> — and the shape they all share is <a href="/book/practice/adapters">Adapters</a>.</p>

  <h2>The verb surface</h2>
  <p>One canonical surface — no aliases, no legacy spellings — in two halves: <code>life &lt;verb&gt;</code> for the engine's own kernel verbs (the repo · the genepool · the live world), and <code>life &lt;gene&gt; &lt;command&gt;</code> for every command an installed cell contributes.</p>
  <p>The live inventory is whatever <code>life</code> prints — derived from the engine you are running and the cells you actually have, so it can never disagree with itself. The <em>contract</em> is <a href="/book/spec/engine-surface">The engine surface</a>.</p>
  <p><code>life ask</code> is the most important verb. Everything — kernel verbs, local cells, imported genes, the whole genepool — is reachable through that one query, lazily, at the depth the task needs.</p>

  <h2>Incremental adoption</h2>
  <p>Life is additive. The installer drops a <code>.life</code> manifest and the engine into any existing repo; nothing breaks, no existing files change. Add <code>.life</code> files to directories one at a time as you touch them — <code>life ask</code> works as soon as one exists.</p>
  <p><code>life test</code> after cloning a .life IS the setup guide — it shows every declared capability that isn't yet connected. No separate onboarding tooling.</p>
</Docs>
